    {
      "type": "context",
      "id": "lK5gA",
      "x": -278.38568906903765,
      "y": -117.67578125,
      "width": 751,
      "height": 5193,
      "content": "# Checkmate — AI-Powered Test Generation\n\nCheckmate is an AI-powered education platform that helps teachers create high-quality tests in seconds, instead of hours.\n\n## Test Generation\n\nAt the core of Checkmate is our test generation engine, designed to give teachers full control over the structure and content of every test they create.\n\n### How it works\n\nTeachers start by defining the basics of the test:\n\n- **Subject** — the area the test belongs to (e.g. Biology, History, English)\n- **Language** — the language the test should be written in\n- **Difficulty level** — adjusted to the age group and skill level of the class\n\nFrom there, teachers build the test out of **sections**. Each section represents a focused part of the test and consists of:\n\n- **Topic** — what this section should cover (e.g. \"Photosynthesis\" or \"World War II causes\")\n- **Question format** — multiple choice, true/false, short answer, open-ended, fill-in-the-blank, and more\n- **Number of questions** — how many questions this section should contain\n\nThis section-based structure lets teachers mix and match formats within a single test — for example, a 5-question multiple choice warm-up, followed by 3 short-answer questions, and finishing with 2 open-ended essay prompts.\n\n### Grounded in your own materials\n\nTeachers can take it a step further by uploading their own teaching materials — a textbook PDF, lecture notes, or a chapter handout. Checkmate uses the uploaded content as the source of truth, generating questions grounded directly in the material students are actually learning from.\n\nThe AI ensures questions stay accurate, relevant, and pedagogically sound, whether it's a quick quiz on biology basics or a comprehensive final exam built from an entire course book.\n\n### Full editorial control\n\nAI generation is just the starting point — teachers always have the final say. Once a test is generated, every question can be manually edited, removed, or replaced, and new questions can be added at any time. Teachers can fine-tune wording, swap out answer options, adjust difficulty, or reorder content until the test perfectly matches their classroom.\n\n### Built for both teachers and students\n\nThe same source content powers two different experiences. Teachers receive a fully structured **test** — ready to assign, print, and grade. Students get **flashcards** generated from the same material, turning the lesson into an interactive study tool they can use to review key concepts, memorize definitions, and prepare for the test at their own pace.\n\nOne source of truth, two tailored outputs — assessment for the classroom, learning for the student.\n\n### Export and share\n\nOnce the test is ready, teachers can download it as a print-ready PDF with a single click — perfectly formatted and ready to hand out in class or share digitally with students.\n\nNo templates. No repetition. Just smarter tests, ready to print or share.\n\n## Marketplace\n\nBeyond test generation, Checkmate is also a community of teachers sharing what they create. Every test generated on the platform is published to the **marketplace**, where any teacher can browse a growing library of ready-made tests across subjects, topics, and grade levels.\n\nTeachers can use marketplace tests in two ways:\n\n- **Use right away** — found a test that fits your lesson? Assign it to your class instantly, no setup required.\n- **Copy and customize** — start from someone else's test as a foundation, then tweak it to match your students. Edit questions, swap formats, adjust difficulty, or add new sections — your modified version becomes your own.\n\nThe marketplace turns individual effort into collective value: every test created strengthens the library, and every teacher saves time by building on the work of others. Instead of starting from a blank page, teachers start from the best of what their peers have already made.\n\n## Technical Details\n\n### Entity Model\n\nThe platform is built around a small set of core entities that together describe how a test is configured, generated, and stored.\n\n#### Test\n\nThe central entity of the platform. A test represents a single generation request and its result.\n\n- **id** — unique identifier (UUID)\n- **status** — lifecycle state: `pending` (queued for generation), `completed` (ready to use), or `failed` (generation error)\n- **test_request** — the original configuration submitted by the user\n- **test_response** — the generated content, populated only when status is `completed`\n\nTests are generated **asynchronously**: a creation request is enqueued and returns a test ID immediately, while the actual generation happens in the background.\n\n#### GenerationRequest\n\nThe configuration that drives test generation. It captures everything the AI needs to produce the test.\n\n- **subject** — the overall subject of the test (e.g. \"English Grammar\", \"Mathematics\")\n- **language** — output language for the test content (defaults to English)\n- **difficulty_level** — `beginner`, `intermediate`, or `advanced`\n- **groups** — list of question groups that make up the test\n\n#### Group\n\nA logical section of the test. Each test consists of one or more groups, and every group produces a homogeneous batch of questions.\n\n- **name** — display name of the group (e.g. \"Present Tenses\")\n- **type** — the question format used for all questions in this group\n- **amount** — number of questions to generate (1–20)\n- **topic** — optional topic override, falls back to the test's subject\n- **rag** — optional RAG configuration for grounding questions in uploaded material\n\n#### Material\n\nAn uploaded reference document (PDF) that can be used as a source of truth for question generation via RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation).\n\n- **material_id** — unique identifier (UUID)\n- **filename** — original file name\n- **status** — `processing` (chunking + embedding in progress), `ready` (usable in generation), or `failed`\n- **chunk_count** / **page_count** — populated once ingestion is complete\n- **expires_at** — automatic expiration (24-hour TTL)\n\nUploads are **deduplicated by content hash** — re-uploading the same file refreshes its TTL instead of re-processing it.\n\n#### RAG Configuration\n\nWhen attached to a group, this binds question generation to a specific uploaded material rather than the model's general knowledge.\n\n- **material_id** — reference to a `ready` material\n- **topic** — optional similarity-search query override\n- **num_chunks** — number of chunks to retrieve (1–20, default 6); lower values produce focused passage-specific questions, higher values produce broader coverage\n\n#### Question\n\nThe generated unit of content. Checkmate supports **ten question types**, each with its own answer model:\n\n- **MCQ_SINGLE** — multiple choice with exactly one correct option\n- **MCQ_MULTIPLE** — multiple choice with two or more correct options\n- **TRUE_FALSE** — boolean statement\n- **OPEN_ENDED** — free-form answer evaluated against expected keywords\n- **FILL_IN** — sentence with a blank, answered via 4 multiple-choice options\n- **ORDERING** — items to be placed in the correct sequence\n- **PAIRS** — matching items between two sets\n- **CATEGORIZATION** — items to be sorted into categories\n- **SHORT_ANSWER** — free-form answer matched against a list of acceptable answers\n- **NUMERIC** — numeric answer with optional tolerance\n\n#### GenerationResponse\n\nThe output produced once generation completes. It mirrors the request structure but contains the actual generated content along with execution metadata.\n\n- **metadata** — generated test name, suggested time limit, topic, level, language\n- **groups** — successfully generated question groups\n- **failed_groups** — any groups that could not be generated after retries (with error reason)\n- **model_id**, **generation_time_ms**, **total_retries** — execution metrics\n\nThe response model tolerates **partial success**: if one group fails, the rest of the test is still returned, and failures are reported explicitly per group rather than failing the whole request.\n\n### Entity Relationships\n\n```\nTest\n ├── GenerationRequest (input)\n │    └── Group [1..N]\n │         └── RAG Config (optional) ──► Material\n │\n └── GenerationResponse (output, when completed)\n      ├── Metadata\n      ├── Group [0..N]  (successful, each with Questions)\n      └── FailedGroup [0..N]  (with error details)\n```\n\n## Style Guide\n\n### Brand Personality\n\nCheckmate is **friendly, warm, and approachable**. The visual identity is built around a single idea: education that feels human, not clinical. Soft pastels, rounded forms, and hand-drawn outlines convey care and curiosity — a deliberate counterpoint to the cold, generic look most assessment software defaults to.\n\n### Mascot\n\nThe Checkmate mascot is the heart of the brand — a wide-eyed, curious character that appears across the product as a guide, helper, and quiet companion to both teachers and students.\n\n**Anatomy**\n\n- **Round, soft silhouette** — approachable, never sharp\n- **Large expressive eyes** — bright cream-white with strong black pupils; the focal point of every variant\n- **Soft cheek blush** — a textured, blurred color blob behind the face that gives the mascot warmth and dimension\n- **Hand-drawn outlines** — 4px strokes with rounded line caps, never mechanical or perfectly aligned\n- **Subtle eyebrow strokes** — small curved lines above each eye that carry the mascot's expression\n\n**Mood variants**\n\nThe mascot ships in four color variants, each tied to a different mood or context within the product:\n\n| Variant | Cheek accent | Background | Tone |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Warm Yellow** | `#F2C181` | `#F6E1C6` | Default — energetic, optimistic |\n| **Sky Blue** | `#9BBBE3` | `#CFE0F4` | Calm, informational, focused |\n| **Olive Green** | `#B3B47D` | `#E2E3CA` | Steady, success, growth |\n| **Coral Pink** | `#F0B08C` | `#F3DACC` | Playful, friendly, encouraging |\n\n### Color Palette\n\n**Base colors** — the foundation of every screen\n\n- **Cream Background** `#FDFAF6` — warm off-white that softens the entire interface; used as the primary canvas\n- **Deep Brown** `#351C07` — used for text, outlines, and strong contrast; warmer and more inviting than pure black\n\n**Accent colors** — used for cheeks, highlights, and category-coding\n\n- Warm Yellow `#F2C181`\n- Sky Blue `#9BBBE3`\n- Olive Green `#B3B47D`\n- Coral Pink `#F0B08C`\n\n**Background tints** — softer pastel shades paired with each accent\n\n- Cream Yellow `#F6E1C6`\n- Soft Blue `#CFE0F4`\n- Light Olive `#E2E3CA`\n- Soft Peach `#F3DACC`\n\n### Visual Language\n\n- **Rounded everything** — corners, line caps, shapes; nothing is sharp or angular\n- **Soft, never saturated** — pastels and warm neutrals; avoid pure white and pure black\n- **Hand-drawn feel** — strokes have a sketched quality, slight asymmetry is welcome\n- **Warm contrast** — Deep Brown on Cream instead of black on white\n- **Generous space** — uncrowded layouts; the content breathes\n- **Texture over flatness** — soft blurs and subtle noise (like the mascot's cheek) keep the design from feeling sterile\n\n### Tone of Voice\n\nThe visual language is matched by the writing voice:\n\n- **Encouraging, not corporate** — \"Let's build your test\" beats \"Configure assessment parameters\"\n- **Plain language** — short sentences, everyday words, no jargon\n- **Teacher-first** — speaks to the educator's reality, respects their time\n- **Quietly playful** — a light touch of warmth, never forced or cutesy\n"
    },
